SCHEDULE 5.07(b)

REGULATION

 This Schedule sets forth the names of the Developed Businesses that are subject to regulation by Governmental Authorities of the tariffs charged by, and other operational matters relating to, them, as well as the names of the appropriate Governmental Authorities exerting such regulatory authority: [Do we need or want this introductory paragraph?  Does it unnecessarily limit the disclosures?]  

 Batangas - The tariff charged by Batangas Power Corp. is regulated by contract and therefore is not subject to further regulatory approval.
  

 Subic - The tariff charged by Subic Power Corp. is set by contract and therefore is not subject to further regulatory approval.  

 
 Guam - The tariff charged by Marianas Energy is set by contract and therefore is not subject to further regulatory approval. 


 Chengdu - The tariff charged by the joint venture received preliminary approval by the provincial pricing bureau and the State Development and Planning Commission (SDPC).  Once the project is operational, application for final tariff approval will be made to the provincial pricing bureau and the SDPC.  Tariffs are reviewed once a year by the provincial pricing bureau and may be adjusted upward (based on increased costs July 17, 2000 and subject to an average grid price) or downward.
   

 SK-Enron - The tariff charged by each of the nine city gas company subsidiaries of SK-Enron is regulated by a Governmental Authority.  Each city gas company’s tariff is subject to guidelines promulgated by the Ministry of Commerce, Industry and Energy (“MOCIE”), and administered by the relevant provincial government, except for Daehan City Gas Co. (which is subject to the Seoul City Council) and Pusan City Gas Co. (which is subject to the Pusan City government).  LPG rates for certain residential and commercial customers are regulated by MOCIE (SK-Enron subsidiary SK Gas is an LPG marketer).  Rates for electricity and steam sales by SK-Enron subsidiary Iksan Energy is also regulated by Governmental Authority.
  

 Bulacan - The tariff is subject to the approval of the Energy Regulatory Board of the Philippines.

 Nigeria - The tariff charged by Enron Nigeria Power is set by contract and therefore is not subject to further regulatory approval.  

